// New arrivals: books bought in for the rooms that were short of them, each with a librarian's note.
//
//   secret     the west-wing bookcase that swings aside (four full columns, all about secrets and hidden rooms)
//   shelves    the general bookcases, in the places that used to repeat a dozen books to fill their gaps
//   evening-*  the Evening Room: quick (under 20 minutes), hour (up to an hour), evening (one to two hours)
//   learners-* the English Reading Room: 1 gentle, 2 steady, 3 richer, 4 challenging, short (the table)
//   signal     the reading room at the Signal House (the night railway)
//   tide       the reading room at Tidebound Quay (the night railway)
//   mars       the Reading Room of Helium, on Mars (mars.js)
//   periodicals the Periodicals Room, beneath the clock in the Grand Hall (periodicals-room.js)
//   spanish    the Spanish Reading Room in the International Wing (international-wing.js): Spanish texts, notes in Spanish
//   portuguese the Portuguese Reading Room, through the green door of the Spanish room: Portuguese texts and notes
//   chinese    the Chinese Reading Room, through the red door of the Spanish room: Chinese texts, notes in traditional
//              Chinese, authors written 曹雪芹 (Cao Xueqin) so the pinyin in Gutenberg's header can be checked
//
// Each entry is [Gutenberg number or null, title, author, category, room, note]. The numbers are only a first
// guess: scripts/new-books.mjs (run by .github/workflows/new-books.yml) checks every one against the text's own
// title and author, looks up the ones that are wrong or missing, bundles the texts into texts/bundled-gzip and
// counts their words. It writes what it found to data/new-books-resolved.js, and only books found there appear.
window.ATHENAEUM_NEW_BOOKS=[
  // ---------- the secret bookcase: secrets, disguises and rooms that should not exist ----------
  [155,'The Moonstone','Wilkie Collins','Mystery','secret','A cursed Indian diamond vanishes from a Yorkshire country house on the night of a birthday party, and the household tells the story in turns, each witness a little less reliable than the last. T. S. Eliot called it the first, the longest and the best of modern English detective novels. Sergeant Cuff and his roses are worth the journey alone.'],
  [583,'The Woman in White','Wilkie Collins','Mystery','secret','A drawing master walking home across Hampstead Heath at midnight meets a young woman dressed all in white who has escaped from an asylum. What follows is a conspiracy of swapped identities and forged records, and one of fiction’s great villains, Count Fosco, with his white mice. Collins tells it in the voices of many witnesses, as if assembling evidence for a trial.'],
  [558,'The Thirty-Nine Steps','John Buchan','Adventure','secret','Richard Hannay is bored in London until a stranger dies in his flat, leaving a notebook full of code and a plot against the country. He spends the rest of the book running across the Scottish moors with half the spies in Europe behind him. Buchan called it a shocker; it is also the model for nearly every man-on-the-run thriller since.'],
  [2360,'The Riddle of the Sands','Erskine Childers','Sea','secret','Two young Englishmen go duck-shooting in a small yacht among the sandbanks of the Frisian coast and find the Germans rehearsing something they should not. Published in 1903, it was read as a warning and taken seriously in Whitehall. The fog, the tides and the charts are as much a part of the plot as the spies.'],
  [95,'The Prisoner of Zenda','Anthony Hope','Adventure','secret','An idle Englishman on holiday in the little kingdom of Ruritania discovers he is the exact double of its king, just as the king is drugged on the eve of his coronation. Duty, disguise and a doomed romance follow, at the point of a sword. It invented a whole country, and gave the language a word for imaginary little kingdoms.'],
  [8954,'Lady Audley’s Secret','Mary Elizabeth Braddon','Gothic','secret','A golden-haired governess marries a wealthy baronet, and his nephew begins to notice that a friend vanished while visiting the house. Braddon’s sensation novel was a scandalous bestseller in 1862. The secret is less shocking now than the question of why the heroine felt she had to keep it.'],
  [60,'The Scarlet Pimpernel','Baroness Orczy','Adventure','secret','During the Terror, a band of Englishmen rescues French aristocrats from the guillotine, led by a man known only by the small red flower he signs his notes with. At home in London, Sir Percy Blakeney appears to be the dullest fop in society. The hero with a foolish public face begins, more or less, here.'],
  [434,'The Circular Staircase','Mary Roberts Rinehart','Mystery','secret','A sensible spinster rents a country house for the summer, and on the first weekend a man is shot dead at the foot of its spiral staircase. Rinehart’s heroine keeps wondering what she might have prevented, which gave rise to the “had I but known” school of mysteries. There is a hidden room, of course; this shelf would not have it otherwise.'],
  [1872,'The Red House Mystery','A. A. Milne','Mystery','secret','Before Winnie-the-Pooh, A. A. Milne wrote one detective novel: a brother back from Australia, a shot behind a locked office door, and two young friends playing Holmes and Watson in a country house. It is light, clever and cheerfully aware of its own genre. There is also a secret passage, which is why it lives here.'],
  [4047,'The Leavenworth Case','Anna Katharine Green','Mystery','secret','A wealthy New York merchant is found shot in his library, and suspicion falls on one of his two beautiful nieces. Published in 1878, nine years before Sherlock Holmes, it was one of the first detective novels written by a woman, and a huge bestseller. Agatha Christie read it as a girl and never forgot it.'],
  [null,'The Big Bow Mystery','Israel Zangwill','Mystery','secret','A man is found with his throat cut in a bedroom locked and bolted from the inside, in a lodging house in the East End of London. Zangwill’s short novel of 1891 is often called the first full-length locked-room mystery. The solution is outrageous, and entirely fair.'],
  [2759,'The Man in the Iron Mask','Alexandre Dumas','Adventure','secret','The ageing musketeers discover the most dangerous secret in France: a prisoner in the Bastille whose face must never be seen, because it is the face of the king. Dumas built the story on a real prisoner of Louis XIV whose identity is still argued over. It is the last part of the musketeers’ long saga, and the saddest.'],
  [null,'Trent’s Last Case','E. C. Bentley','Mystery','secret','An American financier is found shot in the garden of his English country house, and the painter and amateur detective Philip Trent solves it with dazzling logic. Bentley wrote it in 1913 partly to show that great detectives could be fallible. Say no more; the title is a clue.'],
  [974,'The Secret Agent','Joseph Conrad','Conscience','secret','Mr Verloc keeps a shabby shop in Soho, spies for a foreign embassy, and is ordered to blow up the Greenwich Observatory. Conrad drew on a real bombing of 1894 and told it in a voice of cold irony. It is the quietest and most devastating book on this shelf, and much of it is about the people nobody tells the secret to.'],
  [1685,'The Mystery of the Yellow Room','Gaston Leroux','Mystery','secret','A scream in the night, a shot, and a young woman found injured in a room locked from the inside, with her attacker nowhere to be found. Before The Phantom of the Opera, Leroux wrote this classic locked-room puzzle, solved by an eighteen-year-old reporter. The floor plans are part of the fun.'],
  [14851,'Uncle Silas','J. Sheridan Le Fanu','Gothic','secret','When her father dies, young Maud Ruthyn is sent to live with her uncle Silas, a man once suspected of murder, in his decaying house in Derbyshire. Le Fanu turns the terms of a will into a slow-closing trap. Madame de la Rougierre, the governess, is one of the most unsettling figures in Victorian fiction.'],

  // ---------- the general bookcases ----------
  [2814,'Dubliners','James Joyce','Modern','shelves','Fifteen stories of ordinary Dublin lives, beginning in childhood and ending with “The Dead”, one of the finest stories in English. Joyce said he wrote them in “a style of scrupulous meanness”. The last pages, with snow falling all over Ireland, deserve to be read slowly.'],
  [4217,'A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man','James Joyce','Modern','shelves','Stephen Dedalus grows up in Catholic Ireland, and the language of the book grows up with him, from nursery talk to the arguments of a proud young aesthete. It ends with Stephen leaving to forge “the uncreated conscience of my race”. The sermon on hell in the middle is famously terrifying.'],
  [541,'The Age of Innocence','Edith Wharton','Society','shelves','In the New York of the 1870s, Newland Archer is engaged to the perfect May Welland and falls in love with her unconventional cousin, the Countess Olenska. Wharton won the Pulitzer Prize for it in 1921, the first woman to do so. Every dinner party in it is a battle fought with good manners.'],
  [284,'The House of Mirth','Edith Wharton','Society','shelves','Lily Bart is beautiful, twenty-nine, unmarried and running out of money, in a society that values only the first and last of those. Wharton follows her with great sympathy and no mercy at all. It is as sharp about money as anything by Balzac.'],
  [2610,'Notre-Dame de Paris','Victor Hugo','History','shelves','In medieval Paris, the bell-ringer Quasimodo, the dancer Esmeralda and the archdeacon Frollo circle around the great cathedral, which is the real hero of the book. Hugo wrote it partly to stop Gothic buildings being pulled down, and it helped. English readers know it as The Hunchback of Notre-Dame.'],
  [2542,'A Doll’s House','Henrik Ibsen','Drama','shelves','Nora Helmer seems the perfect cheerful wife, until a signature she forged years ago comes back to call. The final scene, and the door that closes at the end of it, caused a sensation across Europe in 1879. It is short enough for one evening and is still argued over.'],
  [600,'Notes from the Underground','Fyodor Dostoyevsky','Conscience','shelves','A retired official in St Petersburg speaks to us from his “underground”, raging against reason, progress and, above all, himself. Short, bitter and in places very funny, it is often called the first existentialist novel. You will not like the narrator; you may recognise him.'],
  [7849,'The Trial','Franz Kafka','Strange','shelves','Josef K. is arrested one morning, on his thirtieth birthday, without having done anything wrong, and is never told the charge. Kafka left the novel unfinished and asked for it to be burned; his friend Max Brod published it instead. The parable “Before the Law”, told inside it, is worth reading twice.'],
  [3825,'Pygmalion','George Bernard Shaw','Drama','shelves','A professor of phonetics bets that he can pass off a Covent Garden flower seller as a duchess by teaching her to speak “properly”. Shaw’s comedy is far sharper about class than the musical it later became. Eliza, in the end, is the one who has learned something.'],
  [2413,'Madame Bovary','Gustave Flaubert','Society','shelves','Emma Bovary, married to a dull country doctor, has read too many romantic novels and tries to live like their heroines, with lovers and debts. Flaubert was put on trial for its immorality in 1857, and acquitted. He spent years on its sentences, and it shows.'],
  [1079,'The Life and Opinions of Tristram Shandy, Gentleman','Laurence Sterne','Comedy','shelves','The narrator sets out to tell the story of his life and does not manage to be born until the third volume. Sterne’s novel includes a black page, a marbled page and a great many digressions about noses. It is proof that experimental fiction is as old as the novel itself.'],
  [6593,'History of Tom Jones, a Foundling','Henry Fielding','Comedy','shelves','A baby is found in a squire’s bed, grows up handsome, good-hearted and forever in trouble, and is turned out onto the roads of eighteenth-century England. Fielding talks to his reader at the start of every book, like a host at a long dinner. It is one of the great comic novels in the language.'],
  [2130,'Utopia','Thomas More','Philosophy','shelves','A traveller describes an island where property is shared, gold is used for chamber pots, and everyone works six hours a day. More wrote it in Latin in 1516, and gave us the word, which means “no place”. Whether he meant any of it seriously has been argued about ever since.'],
  [1041,'Shakespeare’s Sonnets','William Shakespeare','Poetry','shelves','A hundred and fifty-four sonnets on love, time, beauty and betrayal, addressed to a fair young man and a mysterious dark lady. Nobody knows who either of them was, which has never stopped anyone guessing. Start with 18, 29 and 116, and then read the rest.'],
  [5000,'The Notebooks of Leonardo da Vinci','Leonardo da Vinci','Wonder','shelves','Leonardo’s notes on painting, anatomy, flight, water, light and a thousand other things, translated from his mirror-writing. They read like the thoughts of a man who could not stop asking how everything worked. Keep it by you and open it anywhere.'],
  [58585,'The Prophet','Kahlil Gibran','Philosophy','shelves','A prophet about to sail home is asked by the people of the city to speak of love, marriage, children, work, joy and sorrow, and he answers each in turn. Published in 1923, it has never been out of print. It is short, and best read one chapter at a time.'],
  [786,'Hard Times','Charles Dickens','Society','shelves','In the smoky mill town of Coketown, Mr Gradgrind raises his children on facts and nothing but facts, and learns too late what that has cost them. It is the shortest of Dickens’s novels and his angriest about industrial England. The circus people are the only ones with any sense.'],
  [2638,'The Idiot','Fyodor Dostoyevsky','Conscience','shelves','Prince Myshkin, gentle, honest and just back from a Swiss sanatorium, arrives in St Petersburg society and is taken for a fool. Dostoyevsky wanted to portray a truly good man and see what the world would do to him. The answer is not comforting, but the book is unforgettable.'],
  [64317,'The Great Gatsby','F. Scott Fitzgerald','Modern','shelves','Nick Carraway rents a house on Long Island next to the mansion of Jay Gatsby, who throws enormous parties for the sake of one woman across the bay. It came into the public domain in the United States in 2021. The green light at the end of the dock is still shining.'],
  [63107,'Mrs. Dalloway','Virginia Woolf','Modern','shelves','One June day in London in 1923: Clarissa Dalloway buys the flowers herself and prepares for her party, while across the city a shell-shocked soldier, Septimus Smith, struggles to hold on. Woolf moves between their minds as Big Ben strikes the hours. The two never meet, and they are the book’s two halves.'],
  [2383,'The Canterbury Tales','Geoffrey Chaucer','Legend','shelves','A company of pilgrims riding to Canterbury agrees to tell stories on the way, with a free supper as the prize. Knights, millers, nuns and the much-married Wife of Bath all speak in their own voices, some of them rudely. Read it aloud if you can; it was made to be heard.'],
  [228,'The Aeneid','Virgil','Epic','shelves','Aeneas escapes burning Troy with his father on his back and his son by the hand, and sails to find a new home in Italy. Virgil wrote it as the founding story of Rome and died before he had finished revising it. The love of Dido, queen of Carthage, in the fourth book, is the part everyone remembers.'],

  // ---------- the Evening Room ----------
  [7256,'The Gift of the Magi','O. Henry','Society','evening-quick','A young couple in New York with one dollar and eighty-seven cents between them each secretly sells their most precious possession to buy the other a Christmas present. O. Henry is said to have written it in a few hours at a table in a New York tavern. The ending is famous, and it still lands.'],
  [12122,'The Monkey’s Paw','W. W. Jacobs','Ghosts','evening-quick','An old soldier back from India brings a dried monkey’s paw to the Whites’ fireside and warns them that it grants three wishes, at a price. They wish anyway. Jacobs wrote mostly comic stories about sailors; this one has frightened readers for over a century.'],
  [375,'An Occurrence at Owl Creek Bridge','Ambrose Bierce','History','evening-quick','A man stands on a railway bridge in Alabama with a noose around his neck, and the rope breaks. Bierce, who fought in the Civil War himself, tells the rest in a handful of pages. Do not skip to the end.'],
  [11231,'Bartleby, the Scrivener','Herman Melville','Strange','evening-hour','A Wall Street lawyer hires a pale, neat, quiet copyist who, asked one day to check a document, replies: “I would prefer not to.” After that he prefers not to do anything else. Melville’s story is funny, then strange, then very sad.'],
  [60976,'Rip Van Winkle','Washington Irving','Legend','evening-hour','A good-natured idler wanders into the Catskill Mountains, drinks with some strange little men playing ninepins, and sleeps for twenty years, straight through the American Revolution. Irving took an old German folk tale and made it American. Rip’s bewilderment on waking is the best part.'],
  [8147,'The Man Who Would Be King','Rudyard Kipling','Adventure','evening-hour','Two former soldiers and chancers, Dravot and Carnehan, set out from India to make themselves kings of Kafiristan, and very nearly succeed. Kipling frames it as a tale told to a newspaperman on a hot night. It is an adventure, a joke and a judgement on empire all at once.'],
  [902,'The Happy Prince, and Other Tales','Oscar Wilde','Wonder','evening-evening','Five fairy tales Wilde wrote for his sons, among them the golden statue who gives his jewels to the poor with the help of a swallow, and “The Selfish Giant”. They are written for children and read best by grown-ups. Keep a handkerchief nearby for the swallow.'],
  [1093,'The Beast in the Jungle','Henry James','Strange','evening-evening','John Marcher is sure that something rare and terrible is waiting to happen to him, and a woman agrees to wait for it beside him, for years. James’s long story is about a life spent waiting for life to begin. Its last scene, in a cemetery, is one of the most painful he ever wrote.'],
  [1213,'The Man That Corrupted Hadleyburg','Mark Twain','Satire','evening-evening','The town of Hadleyburg is proud of being incorruptible, until a stranger it once insulted leaves behind a sack of gold and a riddle. Twain’s story is a perfect trap, and every respectable citizen walks into it. It is one of his darkest pieces, and one of his funniest.'],
  [844,'The Importance of Being Earnest','Oscar Wilde','Comedy','evening-evening','Two young men invent imaginary people to escape their duties, and both fall for women determined to marry someone called Ernest. Wilde called it “a trivial comedy for serious people”. It may be the most quotable play in English, and it reads in about an hour and a half.'],
  [null,'Benito Cereno','Herman Melville','Sea','evening-evening','An American sealing captain boards a battered Spanish slave ship off the coast of Chile and is welcomed by its nervous, sickly captain and his devoted servant. Nothing aboard is quite what it seems. Melville’s short novel is a masterpiece of suspense, and a searching look at everything its narrator cannot see.'],

  // ---------- the English Reading Room ----------
  [11339,'Aesop’s Fables','Aesop','Legend','learners-1','Hundreds of tiny stories, most only a paragraph long: the tortoise and the hare, the boy who cried wolf, the fox and the grapes. Each one ends with its lesson, so you always know what the story meant. Perfect for reading one or two a day.'],
  [500,'The Adventures of Pinocchio','Carlo Collodi','Wonder','learners-1','A wooden puppet carved by old Geppetto comes to life, and his nose grows every time he tells a lie. He runs away, is tricked, is swallowed by an enormous fish and much more, before he learns to be good. The chapters are short and full of action.'],
  [null,'The Tale of Benjamin Bunny','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-1','Peter Rabbit’s cousin Benjamin takes him back into Mr. McGregor’s garden to fetch the clothes Peter lost there. It goes about as well as you would expect. A very short story in clear, simple English, with a good fright in the middle.'],
  [null,'The Adventures of Buster Bear','Thornton W. Burgess','Wonder','learners-1','Buster Bear comes to live in the Green Forest and learns to catch fish, while Little Joe Otter wonders who is stealing his. Burgess wrote hundreds of these gentle animal stories for children. The sentences are short and friendly, and you learn a little about real animals too.'],
  [271,'Black Beauty','Anna Sewell','Society','learners-2','A horse tells his own life story, from a happy meadow in the country to the hard streets of London, pulling cabs. Anna Sewell wrote it so that people would treat horses more kindly, and it helped to change how they were treated. The language is plain and clear, and the feelings are strong.'],
  [1450,'Pollyanna','Eleanor H. Porter','Society','learners-2','Orphaned Pollyanna goes to live with her strict aunt and plays “the glad game”: finding something to be glad about in everything. Slowly the whole town changes around her. It is warm and simple, and it gave English a word for a cheerful optimist.'],
  [770,'The Story of the Treasure Seekers','E. Nesbit','Wonder','learners-2','The six Bastable children decide to restore their family’s fortune by digging for treasure, becoming detectives, and selling their poetry to a newspaper. One of them tells the story and tries very hard to hide which one. It is very funny, and the English is lively without being hard.'],
  [157,'Daddy-Long-Legs','Jean Webster','Society','learners-3','An orphan girl is sent to college by a rich man she has never met, on one condition: she must write to him every month. The whole book is her letters, full of jokes, little drawings and the new words she is learning. Letters are short, so it is easy to read a little at a time.'],
  [47,'Anne of Avonlea','L. M. Montgomery','Society','learners-3','Anne Shirley, now sixteen, becomes the teacher at the village school and finds that her pupils have as much imagination as she does. If you enjoyed Anne of Green Gables, this is the next step. The descriptions of Prince Edward Island are full of useful words for the natural world.'],
  [498,'Rebecca of Sunnybrook Farm','Kate Douglas Wiggin','Society','learners-3','Talkative, imaginative Rebecca is sent from her family’s poor farm to live with two stern aunts in a small town in Maine. She wins over almost everyone, eventually even the aunts. Her way of speaking is full of expressions worth collecting.'],
  [4276,'North and South','Elizabeth Gaskell','Society','learners-4','Margaret Hale moves from the green south of England to the smoky northern mill town of Milton, and clashes with the proud mill owner John Thornton. Gaskell writes about strikes, class and love all at once. It asks for patience and gives a great deal back.'],
  [6688,'The Mill on the Floss','George Eliot','Society','learners-4','Clever, passionate Maggie Tulliver grows up beside the river Floss with her brother Tom, whom she loves more than anyone. Eliot remembers childhood as well as any writer ever has. The sentences are long but worth it, and the ending is unforgettable.'],
  [7256,'The Gift of the Magi','O. Henry','Society','learners-short',''],
  [null,'The Tale of Tom Kitten','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-short','Mrs. Tabitha Twitchit dresses her three kittens in their best clothes for a tea party and tells them to keep clean. They do not. A five-minute story with easy sentences and some wonderful words, like “pinafore” and “tippet”.'],
  [null,'The Tale of Jemima Puddle-Duck','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-short','A duck who wants to hatch her own eggs goes looking for a quiet nest and meets a very polite gentleman with black ears and a bushy tail. Every reader can see what he is, except Jemima. Short, simple and funny.'],

  // ---------- the night railway: the Signal House reading room ----------
  [null,'The Man in Lower Ten','Mary Roberts Rinehart','Mystery','signal','A young lawyer carrying important papers wakes in the wrong berth of a night sleeper and finds the man in his own berth, lower ten, murdered. Then the train is wrecked. Rinehart’s mystery is fast and funny, and a sleeping car is the ideal place to read it.'],
  [null,'Stories of the Railroad','John A. Hill','Adventure','signal','Tales of American railroad men in the age of steam: runaway engines, snowbound trains and the rivalries of the roundhouse. John A. Hill drove locomotives himself before he became an editor, and later helped found the McGraw-Hill publishing house. He knew exactly what it felt like at the throttle.'],
  [null,'The Wrong Box','Robert Louis Stevenson','Comedy','signal','Two elderly brothers are the last survivors of a tontine, a lottery that pays out to whoever lives longest, and a railway accident sets off a comedy of mislaid bodies. Stevenson wrote it with his stepson, Lloyd Osbourne. It is odd and very funny, and not at all what you expect from the author of Treasure Island.'],
  [null,'The Iron Horse','R. M. Ballantyne','Adventure','signal','Ballantyne’s novel of 1871 about life on the early railway: engine drivers, stokers, signalmen and a runaway train. He liked to learn his subjects first-hand, and his affection for steam is on every page. A signal house seemed the only proper place for it.'],
  [2183,'Three Men on the Bummel','Jerome K. Jerome','Comedy','signal','The three men from the boat, a little older now, take a cycling holiday through Germany, with trains, luggage and a great deal of confusion over the language. Jerome’s observations on the German love of rules are affectionate and very funny. Three Men in a Boat is also in this library, if you would like to start there.'],
  [1298,'The Virginian','Owen Wister','Adventure','signal','A cowboy known only as the Virginian, a schoolteacher from Vermont, and the railway bringing the East to Wyoming. Wister’s novel of 1902 more or less invented the western, including the line “When you call me that, smile!” It begins with the narrator stepping off a train at Medicine Bow.'],
  [null,'The Mystery of the Blue Train','Agatha Christie','Mystery','signal','An American heiress is murdered aboard the luxurious Blue Train from Calais to the Riviera, and her famous rubies are gone. Hercule Poirot happens to be travelling on the same train. Christie wrote it in a hard year and never much liked it, but the train is glorious.'],
  [3177,'Roughing It','Mark Twain','Journey','signal','Young Sam Clemens travels west by stagecoach to Nevada, where he tries silver mining, newspaper work and getting rich, and fails at nearly all of them. It is exaggerated in the best tradition of the tall tale. Before the railway, this is what a long journey felt like.'],

  // ---------- the night railway: the Tidebound Quay reading room ----------
  [2186,'Captains Courageous','Rudyard Kipling','Sea','tide','A spoiled American boy falls off an ocean liner and is picked up by a fishing schooner on the Grand Banks, whose captain will not turn back to port for him. Harvey has to work, and he grows up. Kipling went to Gloucester to learn how the fishermen really lived.'],
  [5658,'Lord Jim','Joseph Conrad','Sea','tide','A young officer abandons a pilgrim ship he believes is sinking, and spends the rest of his life trying to recover from that one moment. Conrad’s Marlow tells the story in pieces, on verandas after dinner. It is a book about honour, and whether a single failure can ever be undone.'],
  [1142,'Typhoon','Joseph Conrad','Sea','tide','Captain MacWhirr, a man with no imagination at all, steers his steamer straight into a typhoon in the China Seas with two hundred passengers below. Conrad, who spent twenty years at sea, wrote the storm from experience. The captain’s stubbornness turns out to be its own kind of courage.'],
  [10743,'Moonfleet','J. Meade Falkner','Adventure','tide','In a Dorset village, young John Trenchard finds a smugglers’ vault beneath the church, and a locket that leads to the lost diamond of Blackbeard. Storms, escapes and a long friendship with an old smuggler follow. It is one of the best adventure stories of the English coast.'],
  [646,'The Coral Island','R. M. Ballantyne','Adventure','tide','Three boys are shipwrecked on a Pacific island and at first live very happily on coconuts and adventure. Ballantyne’s book of 1857 was hugely popular; William Golding wrote Lord of the Flies as a reply to it. Its picture of the Pacific islanders belongs to its time and is not a fair one.'],
  [144,'The Voyage Out','Virginia Woolf','Journey','tide','Rachel Vinrace, twenty-four and sheltered, sails from London to South America on her father’s ship and begins to discover the world and herself. It was Woolf’s first novel. A quay is the right place for a book about leaving.'],
  [null,'Mr. Midshipman Easy','Frederick Marryat','Sea','tide','Jack Easy, raised by a philosopher father to believe that all men are equal, joins the Royal Navy and tries to argue with his captain. Marryat was a naval captain himself and wrote with gusto about life aboard a warship in the Napoleonic Wars. It is lively, silly and full of the sea.'],
  [null,'The Wreck of the Grosvenor','W. Clark Russell','Sea','tide','A young second mate finds the crew of his merchant ship close to mutiny because the food is rotten, and the voyage goes from bad to worse. Clark Russell had been a sailor himself, and this became one of the great Victorian bestsellers of the sea. The storms are very real.'],

  // ---------- Mars: the Reading Room of Helium ----------
  [64,'The Gods of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','John Carter returns to Barsoom and lands in the Valley Dor, the Martian heaven, only to find it is a trap kept by a false religion. Burroughs’s second Mars book is his angriest and one of his most inventive: white apes, plant men and the black pirates of the south. It ends on a cliffhanger that kept its first readers waiting for months.'],
  [68,'The Warlord of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','John Carter chases the villains who have carried off Dejah Thoris across the whole of Barsoom, from the southern pole to the frozen north. It completes the first Barsoom trilogy and is pure momentum. The hothouse cities of the yellow men, under their glass domes in the ice, are among Burroughs’s best inventions.'],
  [null,'The Chessmen of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','Princess Tara of Helium is blown off course in a storm and lands among the Kaldanes, living heads that ride about on headless bodies. Then there is jetan, Martian chess, played with living pieces and to the death. Burroughs printed the rules at the back of the book, so you can play it at home, more gently.'],
  [19141,'Edison’s Conquest of Mars','Garrett P. Serviss','Speculative','mars','Wells’s Martians have been beaten, and the nations of Earth send a fleet of electric ships, designed by Thomas Edison, to strike back. Garrett P. Serviss wrote it as a newspaper serial in 1898, within months of The War of the Worlds and without asking Wells. It has some of the earliest space suits and space battles in fiction.'],
  [10165,'Across the Zodiac','Percy Greg','Speculative','mars','A traveller builds a ship driven by “apergy”, a force that pushes against gravity, and flies to Mars, where he finds an orderly, rather joyless society and a great deal of bureaucracy. Percy Greg’s novel of 1880 is one of the first stories of a voyage to Mars. His ship was called the Astronaut, perhaps the first use of the word.'],
  [47015,'Mars','Percival Lowell','Wonder','mars','The Boston astronomer Percival Lowell built an observatory in Arizona to watch Mars, and saw a network of fine straight lines he believed were canals, dug by a people saving their last water. This is his case, argued carefully and with his own drawings. The canals were not there, but this book fed nearly every Mars story on this shelf.'],
  [null,'Mars as the Abode of Life','Percival Lowell','Wonder','mars','Lowell’s later and bolder book: how planets age, why he thought Mars was older than Earth, and why its inhabitants must be engineers. It is science written as a great argument, and he never gave an inch. Read it beside the canals outside.'],
  [null,'Lieut. Gullivar Jones: His Vacation','Edwin Lester Arnold','Adventure','mars','A lieutenant of the United States Navy unrolls a strange carpet and finds himself on Mars, among a pleasant, idle people and the barbarians who threaten them. Edwin Lester Arnold’s novel of 1905 sank without trace. It is read now as a forerunner of Burroughs’s Barsoom, which appeared seven years later.'],
  [null,'The Certainty of a Future Life in Mars','L. P. Gratacap','Speculative','mars','A father promises his son that after death he will send word from Mars, where he believes souls go next, and the messages arrive by wireless. Louis Pope Gratacap was a curator at the American Museum of Natural History. It is strange and earnest, and it is the reason the wireless set in the ruin is kept switched on.'],

  // ---------- the Periodicals Room: magazines and papers as they first appeared ----------
  [null,'Punch, or the London Charivari, Volume 1, July 17, 1841','Various','Comedy','periodicals','The first volume of Punch, the London comic weekly founded in 1841 by Henry Mayhew and Ebenezer Landells. It gave the word “cartoon” its modern meaning, printed Thackeray, and kept going for a century and a half. Early Punch is rowdier and more radical than its later reputation.'],
  [null,'The Strand Magazine, Vol. 01, Issue 01, January 1891','Various','Mystery','periodicals','The first number of The Strand Magazine. Within six months it was printing short stories about a new detective called Sherlock Holmes, and its sales rose with every one. Its founder, George Newnes, wanted a picture on every page, and very nearly managed it.'],
  [null,'The Atlantic Monthly, Vol. 01, No. 01, November, 1857','Various','Society','periodicals','The first number of The Atlantic Monthly, founded in Boston in 1857 by a circle that included Emerson, Longfellow, Oliver Wendell Holmes and Harriet Beecher Stowe. Its pieces were unsigned, so its first readers had to guess who had written what. The magazine is still published.'],
  [null,'Notes and Queries, Number 01, November 3, 1849','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The first number of Notes and Queries, a weekly founded in 1849 so that readers could put obscure questions to one another and answer them. Its motto came from Captain Cuttle in Dickens’s Dombey and Son: “When found, make a note of.” It is still running, as a scholarly journal.'],
  [null,'The Yellow Book, Volume 1, April 1894','Various','Modern','periodicals','The first volume of The Yellow Book, the notorious quarterly of 1894, with drawings by Aubrey Beardsley and a story by Henry James. It was yellow because French novels were, and French novels were wicked. When Oscar Wilde was arrested he was said to be carrying a yellow book, and the magazine never recovered, although he never wrote for it.'],
  [null,'The Germ: Thoughts towards Nature in Poetry, Literature and Art','Various','Poetry','periodicals','The magazine of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, which lasted four numbers in 1850 and sold almost nothing. It printed Dante Gabriel Rossetti’s The Blessed Damozel and early poems by Christina Rossetti. Few magazines have failed so beautifully.'],
  [null,'The Idler Magazine, Vol. III, May 1893','Various','Comedy','periodicals','The Idler, a monthly founded in 1892 by Jerome K. Jerome and Robert Barr for light, clever reading. Conan Doyle, Kipling, Mark Twain and Bret Harte all wrote for it. The editors’ gossipy column, The Idlers’ Club, was half the reason people bought it.'],
  [null,'St. Nicholas Magazine for Boys and Girls, Vol. 5, No. 5, March, 1878','Various','Wonder','periodicals','St. Nicholas, the great American children’s magazine, edited for over thirty years by Mary Mapes Dodge, the author of Hans Brinker. Louisa May Alcott, Mark Twain and Rudyard Kipling all wrote for it. Generations of American children grew up waiting for it each month.'],
  [null,'Harper\'s Young People, January 20, 1880','Various','Wonder','periodicals','Harper’s Young People, a weekly for children launched in 1879, full of serials, puzzles, games and letters from readers across America. It is a window on what a Victorian childhood was supposed to enjoy, and sometimes did.'],
  [null,'Scientific American, Vol. 17, No. 1, July 6, 1867','Various','Speculative','periodicals','Scientific American in its weekly years, when it was chiefly a journal of inventions: new engines, safety devices and improved machinery, each with an engraving. Its publishers also ran a patent agency, so the inventors came to them. Browse it for the future as 1867 imagined it.'],
  [null,'Blackwood\'s Edinburgh Magazine, Vol. 55, No. 339, January, 1844','Various','Society','periodicals','Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine, “Maga” to its readers: Tory, combative and literary, with fiction, travel and politics sharing the same double columns. George Eliot’s first fiction appeared in its pages in 1857. Its readers argued about it almost as much as they read it.'],
  [null,'Chambers\'s Edinburgh Journal, No. 419, New Series, January 3, 1852','Various','Society','periodicals','Chambers’s Edinburgh Journal, a cheap, respectable weekly of useful knowledge founded by the brothers William and Robert Chambers in 1832. It aimed to give working readers good reading for a penny and a half. It is a fine picture of what ordinary Victorians learned from.'],
  [null,'Godey\'s Lady\'s Book, Vol. 42, January, 1851','Various','Society','periodicals','Godey’s Lady’s Book, the most popular American magazine of the mid-nineteenth century, with hand-coloured fashion plates, recipes, patterns and fiction. Its editor, Sarah Josepha Hale, campaigned for years to make Thanksgiving a national holiday, and won. She also wrote “Mary Had a Little Lamb”.'],
  [null,'The Crisis, Vol. 1, No. 1, November, 1910','Various','Contested','periodicals','The first number of The Crisis, the magazine of the NAACP, founded and edited by W. E. B. Du Bois in 1910. It reported lynching, discrimination and achievement with equal force, and later published the young writers of the Harlem Renaissance. A thousand copies of this first number were printed.'],
  [null,'Astounding Stories of Super-Science, January 1930','Various','Speculative','periodicals','The very first issue of Astounding Stories of Super-Science: pulp paper, a lurid cover and adventure among the planets. Within a decade, as Astounding Science Fiction, it would be the magazine that made modern science fiction. Here it is before it grew up.'],
  [null,'McClure\'s Magazine, Vol. 1, No. 1, June, 1893','Various','Society','periodicals','The first number of McClure’s Magazine, sold for fifteen cents when its rivals cost twenty-five or thirty-five. Within ten years it had helped invent investigative journalism, with Ida Tarbell’s history of Standard Oil. This first number is gentler: interviews, stories and a great deal of hope.'],
  [null,'The Mirror of Literature, Amusement, and Instruction, Vol. 10, No. 287, December 22, 1827','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The Mirror of Literature, Amusement, and Instruction, a twopenny London weekly of the 1820s: engravings, curiosities, extracts from new books and odd facts. It was one of the first cheap illustrated magazines, and it reads like a cabinet of curiosities made of paper.'],
  [null,'The Spectator, Volume 1','Joseph Addison and Richard Steele','Philosophy','periodicals','The Spectator of 1711 and 1712, a daily sheet written chiefly by Joseph Addison and Richard Steele, in which the silent Mr. Spectator watches London from its coffee houses. It set out to bring philosophy “out of closets and libraries” to tea tables and coffee houses. It taught the eighteenth century how to write an essay.'],
  [null,'The Continental Monthly, Vol. 1, No. 1, January, 1862','Various','History','periodicals','The Continental Monthly, founded in 1862, in the middle of the American Civil War, to argue for the Union and against slavery. Its essays and stories were written for readers who knew the war was going on outside their windows.'],
  [null,'Birds, Illustrated by Color Photography, Vol. 1, No. 1, January 1897','Various','Wonder','periodicals','Birds, Illustrated by Color Photography, a Chicago monthly of 1897 and one of the first magazines to print photographs in colour. The birds were mounted specimens posed on branches, which is why some of them look a little startled. Several pieces are written as if the bird itself were speaking.'],
  [null,'The Great Round World and What Is Going On In It, Vol. 1, No. 1, November 4, 1896','Various','History','periodicals','A weekly newspaper for young people, begun in 1896: the week’s news explained plainly, from the war in Cuba to the troubles in Crete. It is the nearest thing in this room to a real newspaper, and a good way to find out what the world talked about that autumn.'],
  [null,'The Nursery, April 1873, Vol. XIII. No. 4','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The Nursery, a Boston monthly for the youngest readers, with large type, short stories and a picture on nearly every page. Simple, gentle and easy to read, it is a good place to start for anyone learning English, too.'],
  // ---------- the International Wing: the Spanish Reading Room (notes in Spanish; texts in Spanish) ----------
  [2000,'Don Quijote','Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra','Comedy','spanish','Un hidalgo de La Mancha lee tantos libros de caballerías que acaba saliendo a buscar aventuras con una lanza vieja, un caballo flaco y un vecino por escudero. Cervantes publicó la primera parte en 1605 y la segunda en 1615, cuando ya circulaba una continuación falsa escrita por otro. Los molinos de viento son solo el principio.'],
  [null,'Novelas ejemplares','Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra','Society','spanish','Doce novelas cortas de 1613, con pícaros sevillanos, una gitanilla, un licenciado que se cree de vidrio y dos perros que conversan una noche en un hospital de Valladolid. En el prólogo, Cervantes presume de ser el primero que ha novelado en lengua castellana. Se pueden leer en cualquier orden, una por noche.'],
  [320,'Lazarillo de Tormes','Anónimo','Satire','spanish','Un muchacho de Salamanca sirve a un ciego, a un clérigo avaro y a un escudero sin un céntimo, y de cada amo aprende una manera nueva de pasar hambre. Se publicó sin nombre de autor en 1554, y todavía nadie sabe con certeza quién lo escribió. Es breve, cruel y muy divertido, y con él empezó la novela picaresca.'],
  [null,'La Celestina','Fernando de Rojas','Drama','spanish','Calisto se enamora de Melibea, y su criado le recomienda una vieja alcahueta capaz de abrir cualquier puerta. Fernando de Rojas, estudiante de leyes en Salamanca, la publicó hacia 1499 y la amplió en ediciones posteriores. Está escrita toda en diálogo, como una obra de teatro demasiado larga para representarse.'],
  [32315,'Historia de la vida del Buscón','Francisco de Quevedo','Satire','spanish','Pablos, hijo de un barbero ladrón y de una madre con fama de bruja, quiere ser caballero y lo intenta por todos los caminos equivocados. Quevedo la escribió de joven y se publicó en 1626. Cada frase trae un chiste, y casi todos son crueles.'],
  [50027,'El sí de las niñas','Leandro Fernández de Moratín','Comedy','spanish','Doña Francisca tiene dieciséis años, y su madre la ha prometido a don Diego, que tiene cincuenta y nueve. Todo se resuelve en una sola noche, en una posada de Alcalá de Henares. Moratín la estrenó en 1806 con un éxito enorme, aunque a la Inquisición no le hizo ninguna gracia.'],
  [17340,'Marianela','Benito Pérez Galdós','Society','spanish','Nela es una muchacha pobre y poco agraciada que sirve de lazarillo a Pablo, un joven ciego que la cree hermosa. Cuando llega un médico capaz de devolverle la vista, todos se alegran menos ella. Galdós la publicó en 1878.'],
  [null,'Misericordia','Benito Pérez Galdós','Society','spanish','Benina, criada de una señora arruinada de Madrid, sale cada día a pedir limosna a escondidas para que su ama pueda seguir comiendo. Galdós recorrió los barrios más pobres de la ciudad para escribirla y la publicó en 1897. Pocas novelas son tan bondadosas sin ponerse nunca blandas.'],
  [16961,'Trafalgar','Benito Pérez Galdós','History','spanish','Gabriel Araceli, un muchacho de Cádiz de catorce años, acaba a bordo del Santísima Trinidad el día de la batalla de 1805. Es el primero de los cuarenta y seis Episodios nacionales con los que Galdós contó el siglo XIX español. La batalla se ve desde la cubierta, entre el miedo y el humo.'],
  [null,'Fortunata y Jacinta','Benito Pérez Galdós','Society','spanish','Juanito Santa Cruz, hijo de comerciantes ricos de Madrid, se casa con su prima Jacinta, pero no deja de volver a Fortunata, una muchacha del pueblo. Galdós la publicó en cuatro partes entre 1886 y 1887. Es larga como una ciudad, y como una ciudad está llena de gente.'],
  [null,'Niebla','Miguel de Unamuno','Philosophy','spanish','Augusto Pérez, un joven rico y desocupado, se enamora de una profesora de piano y empieza a dudar de que él mismo exista. Al final viaja a Salamanca para discutirlo con su autor, un tal Miguel de Unamuno. Se publicó en 1914, y Unamuno la llamó “nivola” para que nadie le explicara cómo se escribe una novela.'],
  [44512,'Abel Sánchez','Miguel de Unamuno','Conscience','spanish','Joaquín y Abel son amigos desde niños, y Joaquín lleva toda la vida envidiándolo. Unamuno la publicó en 1917 con el subtítulo “Una historia de pasión”. Es la historia de Caín contada desde dentro.'],
  [null,'La barraca','Vicente Blasco Ibáñez','Society','spanish','En la huerta de Valencia, los vecinos han jurado que nadie volverá a cultivar las tierras de las que echaron al tío Barret. Cuando una familia forastera se instala en la barraca abandonada, empieza una guerra callada. Blasco Ibáñez la publicó en 1898.'],
  [57781,'Cañas y barro','Vicente Blasco Ibáñez','Society','spanish','En la Albufera de Valencia, entre arrozales y canales, tres generaciones de una familia de pescadores no se ponen de acuerdo sobre cómo ganarse la vida. Blasco Ibáñez la publicó en 1902 y la tenía por su mejor novela. El agua quieta del lago lo oye todo.'],
  [null,'Sangre y arena','Vicente Blasco Ibáñez','Society','spanish','Juan Gallardo, hijo de un zapatero de Sevilla, llega a ser el torero más famoso de España y lo va perdiendo todo por el camino. Blasco Ibáñez la publicó en 1908, y Hollywood la llevó al cine más de una vez. No es una novela a favor de los toros.'],
  [null,'Los cuatro jinetes del Apocalipsis','Vicente Blasco Ibáñez','History','spanish','Los nietos de un estanciero argentino, unos franceses y otros alemanes, se encuentran en bandos contrarios cuando estalla la Primera Guerra Mundial. Blasco Ibáñez la publicó en 1916, en plena guerra, y traducida al inglés fue uno de los libros más vendidos en Estados Unidos. Los cuatro jinetes son la guerra, la peste, el hambre y la muerte.'],
  [null,'Los pazos de Ulloa','Emilia Pardo Bazán','Society','spanish','Julián, un capellán joven y tímido, llega a una casa señorial de Galicia y encuentra al marqués viviendo con su criada, al mayordomo mandando más que el señor y a un niño pequeño al que dan vino para reírse. Emilia Pardo Bazán la publicó en 1886. La lluvia gallega cae sobre todo, y nadie la detiene.'],
  [null,'La Regenta','Leopoldo Alas','Society','spanish','Ana Ozores, casada con un regente jubilado mucho mayor que ella, se aburre en Vetusta, una ciudad de provincias muy parecida a Oviedo. Se la disputan el magistral de la catedral y el donjuán del casino. Clarín la publicó en dos tomos, en 1884 y 1885.'],
  [17223,'Pepita Jiménez','Juan Valera','Society','spanish','Luis de Vargas, un seminarista a punto de ordenarse, pasa unas semanas en el pueblo andaluz de su padre y conoce a la joven viuda con la que su padre piensa casarse. La primera parte son las cartas que escribe a su tío, el deán, cada una un poco más inquieta que la anterior. Valera la publicó en 1874.'],
  [null,'El sombrero de tres picos','Pedro Antonio de Alarcón','Comedy','spanish','El corregidor de un pueblo andaluz se encapricha de la molinera, y una noche intenta aprovechar que el molinero está fuera de casa. Alarcón la publicó en 1874, a partir de un romance popular. Manuel de Falla la convirtió después en un ballet.'],
  [29731,'El capitán Veneno','Pedro Antonio de Alarcón','Comedy','spanish','Un capitán gruñón que no soporta a las mujeres cae herido en las calles de Madrid en 1848 y no tiene más remedio que dejarse cuidar por una viuda y su hija. Alarcón la publicó en 1881. Es una novela corta, y el capitán pierde todas sus batallas.'],
  [23600,'La gaviota','Fernán Caballero','Society','spanish','Un médico alemán se retira a un pueblo de la costa de Huelva y se casa con Marisalada, la hija de un pescador, que canta como nadie. Fernán Caballero era el seudónimo de Cecilia Böhl de Faber, que la publicó en 1849. La voz de la muchacha la llevará a los teatros de Sevilla y de Madrid.'],
  [null,'La hermana San Sulpicio','Armando Palacio Valdés','Comedy','spanish','Un poeta gallego que toma las aguas en un balneario se enamora de una monja andaluza muy alegre que todavía no ha hecho los votos definitivos. La sigue hasta Sevilla, donde la familia de ella no piensa ponérselo fácil. Palacio Valdés la publicó en 1889.'],
  [null,'Azul...','Rubén Darío','Poetry','spanish','Cuentos y poemas que el joven nicaragüense publicó en Valparaíso en 1888, a los veintiún años. Con ellos empezó el modernismo, y el español sonó de pronto de otra manera. Hay princesas y cisnes, y un rey burgués que pone a un poeta a dar vueltas a un organillo.'],
  [null,'Cantos de vida y esperanza','Rubén Darío','Poetry','spanish','Rubén Darío publicó estos poemas en Madrid en 1905, cuando ya era el poeta más famoso de la lengua. Hay una oda “A Roosevelt” que no es un elogio, y un “Nocturno” difícil de olvidar. Son más graves que los de Azul..., y también más suyos.'],
  [14765,'El gaucho Martín Fierro','José Hernández','Poetry','spanish','Un gaucho canta con su guitarra cómo lo llevaron a la fuerza a la frontera, cómo perdió su rancho y su familia, y cómo acabó fuera de la ley. José Hernández lo publicó en 1872, y es el poema nacional de la Argentina. En muchas casas del Río de la Plata se sabe de memoria.'],
  [null,'Facundo','Domingo Faustino Sarmiento','History','spanish','La vida de Facundo Quiroga, caudillo de La Rioja, escrita en 1845 por un argentino exiliado en Chile que quería explicar su país a través de él. Sarmiento llegaría a ser presidente de la Argentina. Es mitad biografía, mitad ensayo y mitad panfleto, y no le sobra ninguna de las tres.'],
  [null,'Tradiciones peruanas','Ricardo Palma','History','spanish','Cientos de historias breves de la Lima de los virreyes, con santos, bandidos y tapadas, contadas con una sonrisa y sin demasiado respeto por las fechas. Ricardo Palma las fue publicando durante décadas a partir de 1872. También dirigió la Biblioteca Nacional del Perú y la reconstruyó, libro a libro, después de la guerra con Chile.'],
  [null,'Cuentos de amor de locura y de muerte','Horacio Quiroga','Ghosts','spanish','Cuentos de la selva de Misiones, donde Quiroga vivió muchos años: un almohadón de plumas, una gallina degollada, un hombre mordido por una víbora que baja el río en canoa. El escritor uruguayo los reunió en 1917. Son perfectos y dan miedo, en ese orden.'],
  [null,'Ariel','José Enrique Rodó','Philosophy','spanish','Un viejo maestro al que sus alumnos llaman Próspero se despide de ellos con un largo discurso sobre la juventud, la belleza y el peligro de admirar demasiado a los Estados Unidos. El uruguayo José Enrique Rodó lo publicó en 1900. Toda una generación de lectores latinoamericanos se reconoció en él.'],
  [47584,'Noli me tangere','José Rizal','Society','spanish','Crisóstomo Ibarra vuelve a Filipinas tras siete años de estudios en Europa y encuentra su pueblo en manos de los frailes y de la guardia civil. José Rizal la escribió en español, la publicó en Berlín en 1887, y las autoridades españolas la prohibieron. Rizal fue fusilado en Manila en 1896, y hoy es el héroe nacional de Filipinas.'],
  [null,'El filibusterismo','José Rizal','Society','spanish','Trece años después de Noli me tangere, un joyero rico llamado Simoun vuelve a Manila con mucho dinero y un plan de venganza. José Rizal la publicó en Gante en 1891. Es más oscura que la primera, y lo es a propósito.'],
  [null,'Sab','Gertrudis Gómez de Avellaneda','Romance','spanish','Sab, un esclavo mulato de una plantación de Cuba, está enamorado en silencio de Carlota, la hija de su amo. Gertrudis Gómez de Avellaneda la publicó en Madrid en 1841, once años antes que La cabaña del tío Tom. Estuvo prohibida en Cuba durante años.'],
  [null,'La edad de oro','José Martí','Wonder','spanish','Una revista para los niños de América que José Martí escribió casi entera él solo, desde Nueva York, en 1889, y que duró cuatro números. Trae cuentos, poemas, la Ilíada contada a los niños y la historia del hombre contada por sus casas. Martí quería que los niños supieran de dónde venían.'],
  [65689,'Las fuerzas extrañas','Leopoldo Lugones','Strange','spanish','Cuentos de 1906 en los que la ciencia se sale de madre: un hombre que quiere ver la música, una lluvia de fuego sobre una ciudad antigua, una estatua de sal que todavía siente. El argentino Leopoldo Lugones fue de los primeros en escribir ciencia ficción en español. Borges lo admiraba y lo discutía casi a partes iguales.'],
  [53552,'Obras escogidas','Gustavo Adolfo Bécquer','Poetry','spanish','Las rimas, las leyendas y las cartas Desde mi celda de Bécquer en un solo tomo: poemas breves sobre el amor y la soledad, y relatos de monjes, caballeros y lugares encantados. El manuscrito de las rimas se perdió en la revolución de 1868, y Bécquer tuvo que reconstruirlas de memoria; sus amigos las publicaron en 1871, al año de su muerte. “¿Qué es poesía? Poesía… eres tú.”'],
  [52392,'Miau','Benito Pérez Galdós','Society','spanish','Ramón Villaamil, un funcionario al que han dejado cesante a dos meses de la jubilación, pasa los días escribiendo cartas a ministerios que no le contestan. A las mujeres de su casa las llaman en el barrio “las Miau”, porque tienen cara de gato. Galdós la publicó en 1888, y es a la vez muy triste y muy graciosa.'],
  [66979,'Tristana','Benito Pérez Galdós','Society','spanish','Tristana, joven y huérfana, vive bajo la tutela de don Lope, un viejo seductor que la ha hecho su amante. Ella sueña con ser libre, con ser artista, con ganarse la vida sola, y se enamora de un pintor. Galdós la publicó en 1892, y Buñuel la llevó al cine en 1970.'],
  [44358,'La tía Tula','Miguel de Unamuno','Conscience','spanish','Cuando muere su hermana, Gertrudis se queda a criar a sus sobrinos y se niega a casarse con el viudo, que la quiere. Quiere ser madre sin ser esposa, y lo consigue a su manera. Unamuno la publicó en 1921.'],
  [18166,'Amistad funesta','José Martí','Romance','spanish','La única novela de José Martí, publicada por entregas en Nueva York en 1885 bajo un seudónimo de mujer. Un joven abogado duda entre dos muchachas, y los celos terminan muy mal. Martí la escribió por encargo y le tenía poco cariño, pero se lee con gusto.'],
  [47650,'Prosas profanas','Rubén Darío','Poetry','spanish','Rubén Darío publicó estos poemas en Buenos Aires en 1896. Aquí está la “Sonatina”, la de “La princesa está triste... ¿qué tendrá la princesa?”, que medio continente aprendió de memoria en la escuela. Cisnes, marquesas y jardines, en un español que nunca había sonado así.'],
  [18600,'Mi último adiós','José Rizal','Poetry','spanish','El poema que José Rizal escribió en su celda de Manila antes de ser fusilado, en diciembre de 1896. Lo escondió en una lamparilla de alcohol que entregó a su familia. Se despide de Filipinas verso a verso.'],
  // ---------- the International Wing: the Portuguese Reading Room (notes in Portuguese; texts in Portuguese, in the spelling of their editions) ----------
  [55752,'Dom Casmurro','Machado de Assis','Romance','portuguese','Já velho, Bento Santiago conta como se apaixonou por Capitu, a vizinha dos “olhos de ressaca”, e como passou a suspeitar que ela o traíra com o seu melhor amigo, Escobar. Machado de Assis publicou-o em 1899. O leitor fecha o livro sem saber a verdade, e é exatamente essa a intenção.'],
  [54829,'Memorias Posthumas de Braz Cubas','Machado de Assis','Satire','portuguese','Um defunto conta a sua vida a partir do túmulo, e dedica o livro ao verme que primeiro lhe roeu as frias carnes do cadáver. Machado de Assis publicou-o em 1881, e com ele o romance brasileiro mudou de rumo. É irónico, livre e muito divertido, sobretudo quando o narrador não tem razão.'],
  [55682,'Quincas Borba','Machado de Assis','Satire','portuguese','Rubião, professor de Barbacena, herda a fortuna do filósofo Quincas Borba com uma condição: cuidar do cão, que também se chama Quincas Borba. Muda-se para o Rio de Janeiro e a fortuna começa a escapar-lhe por entre os dedos. Machado de Assis publicou-o em livro em 1891: “Ao vencedor, as batatas.”'],
  [56737,'Esau e Jacob','Machado de Assis','Society','portuguese','Pedro e Paulo, gémeos que brigam desde o ventre da mãe, crescem um monarquista e o outro republicano, e apaixonam-se pela mesma rapariga, Flora. Tudo se passa no Rio de Janeiro dos anos da proclamação da República. Machado de Assis publicou-o em 1904.'],
  [55797,'Memorial de Ayres','Machado de Assis','Society','portuguese','O diário de um diplomata aposentado, o conselheiro Aires, nos anos de 1888 e 1889, a observar um casal de velhos e uma viúva jovem no Rio de Janeiro. É o último romance de Machado de Assis, publicado em 1908, o ano da sua morte. Quase nada acontece, e tudo é dito com uma delicadeza enorme.'],
  [57001,'Papeis Avulsos','Machado de Assis','Satire','portuguese','Contos de 1882, entre eles “O alienista”, em que o doutor Simão Bacamarte abre um hospício na vila de Itaguaí e acaba por lá internar quase toda a gente. Há também a “Teoria do medalhão”, um pai a ensinar o filho a não ter ideias. Machado de Assis no seu melhor, a sorrir de lado.'],
  [33056,'Historias Sem Data','Machado de Assis','Society','portuguese','Contos de 1884, entre eles “A igreja do diabo”, em que o Diabo decide fundar a sua própria igreja, e “Noite de almirante”, em que um marinheiro volta de viagem e descobre que a namorada não esperou. Machado de Assis chamou-lhes histórias sem data porque podiam passar-se em qualquer tempo. E passam.'],
  [67162,'Helena','Machado de Assis','Romance','portuguese','O conselheiro Vale morre e, no testamento, reconhece uma filha natural, Helena, que vai viver com a família. O filho, Estácio, afeiçoa-se à nova irmã mais do que devia. Machado de Assis publicou-o em 1876, na sua fase romântica.'],
  [61653,'Poesias Completas','Machado de Assis','Poetry','portuguese','Os poemas que o próprio Machado de Assis reuniu em 1901: Crisálidas, Falenas, Americanas e Ocidentais. Antes de ser o grande romancista do Brasil, foi poeta, e nunca deixou de o ser. As Ocidentais, as últimas, são as mais suas.'],
  [67740,'Iracema','José de Alencar','Legend','portuguese','Iracema, a virgem dos lábios de mel, filha do pajé dos tabajaras, apaixona-se por Martim, um guerreiro português. José de Alencar chamou-lhe “lenda do Ceará” e publicou-a em 1865. Há quem repare que Iracema é um anagrama de América.'],
  [67724,'O Guarany, Vol. 1','José de Alencar','Adventure','portuguese','No século XVII, junto ao rio Paquequer, o índio Peri dedica a vida a proteger Ceci, a filha de um fidalgo português. José de Alencar publicou-o em folhetins em 1857, e Carlos Gomes fez dele uma ópera, Il Guarany. Este é o primeiro de dois volumes.'],
  [67725,'O Guarany, Vol. 2','José de Alencar','Adventure','portuguese','O segundo volume de O Guarani: a casa de D. Antônio de Mariz é cercada, e Peri tem de salvar Ceci de tudo ao mesmo tempo. José de Alencar publicou-o em 1857. O final é das coisas mais extraordinárias que o romantismo brasileiro produziu.'],
  [69187,'O Cortiço','Aluísio Azevedo','Society','portuguese','João Romão, um português ganancioso, junta dinheiro tostão a tostão até ser dono de um cortiço cheio de gente no Rio de Janeiro, ao lado do sobrado de um comerciante que o despreza. Aluísio Azevedo publicou-o em 1890. O verdadeiro protagonista é o próprio cortiço, que parece respirar.'],
  [68541,'O Atheneu','Raul Pompeia','Society','portuguese','“Vais encontrar o mundo, disse-me meu pai, à porta do Ateneu.” Sérgio, um menino de onze anos, entra num colégio interno do Rio de Janeiro dirigido pelo vaidoso Aristarco. Raul Pompeia publicou-o em 1888, e a última página arde.'],
  [67535,'Triste Fim de Polycarpo Quaresma','Lima Barreto','Satire','portuguese','O major Policarpo Quaresma ama tanto o Brasil que pede ao Congresso que o tupi passe a ser a língua oficial, e é internado num hospício. Lima Barreto publicou-o em livro em 1915. É engraçado até deixar de o ser.'],
  [74475,'A escrava Isaura','Bernardo Guimarães','Society','portuguese','Isaura é escravizada, embora educada e de pele clara, e o seu senhor, Leôncio, persegue-a sem descanso. Bernardo Guimarães publicou-o em 1875, em plena campanha abolicionista. Cem anos depois, a telenovela tornou-a conhecida em meio mundo.'],
  [68635,'Innocencia','Visconde de Taunay','Romance','portuguese','Cirino, que anda pelo sertão a curar doentes, hospeda-se em casa de Pereira e apaixona-se pela filha dele, Inocência, já prometida a outro. O Visconde de Taunay publicou-o em 1872. O sertão de Mato Grosso é descrito por alguém que o percorreu.'],
  [3333,'Os Lusíadas','Luís de Camões','Epic','portuguese','A viagem de Vasco da Gama à Índia, contada em dez cantos, com o gigante Adamastor no Cabo das Tormentas e a história de Inês de Castro pelo meio. Camões publicou-o em 1572. Segundo a tradição, salvou o manuscrito a nado, num naufrágio na foz do Mekong.'],
  [31971,'O crime do padre Amaro','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Amaro, um padre novo sem vocação, chega a Leiria e apaixona-se por Amélia, a filha da dona da casa onde se hospeda. Eça de Queirós publicou-o em 1875 e reescreveu-o duas vezes. A província, a sacristia e a hipocrisia nunca foram tão bem descritas.'],
  [40409,'Os Maias','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Carlos da Maia, rico, belo e diletante, instala-se com o avô no Ramalhete, em Lisboa, e apaixona-se por uma mulher misteriosa, Maria Eduarda. Eça de Queirós publicou-o em 1888. É o grande romance português do século XIX, e o amigo de Carlos, João da Ega, é das melhores companhias da literatura.'],
  [42942,'O Primo Bazilio','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Luísa, casada e aborrecida, fica sozinha em Lisboa enquanto o marido viaja, e o primo Basílio regressa de Paris. A criada, Juliana, rouba-lhe as cartas, e começa uma chantagem terrível. Eça de Queirós publicou-o em 1878.'],
  [16384,'O Mandarim','Eça de Queirós','Strange','portuguese','Teodoro, um amanuense de Lisboa, lê num livro que basta tocar uma campainha para matar um velho mandarim na China e herdar a sua fortuna. Toca a campainha. Eça de Queirós publicou esta pequena fantasia em 1880.'],
  [17515,'A Relíquia','Eça de Queirós','Satire','portuguese','Teodorico Raposo, sobrinho de uma tia rica e beata, vai a Jerusalém buscar-lhe uma relíquia santa para garantir a herança. O embrulho que traz de volta não é bem o que ela esperava. Eça de Queirós publicou-o em 1887, e pelo meio há um sonho que leva Teodorico à Jerusalém do tempo de Cristo.'],
  [18220,'A Cidade e as Serras','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Jacinto vive em Paris, rodeado de todas as máquinas e confortos do progresso, e aborrece-se de morte. Uma viagem às suas terras de Tormes, nas serras portuguesas, muda-lhe a vida. Publicou-se em 1901, um ano depois da morte de Eça de Queirós.'],
  [23145,'A Illustre Casa de Ramires','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Gonçalo Mendes Ramires, último de uma família mais antiga do que o reino, escreve uma novela sobre os feitos dos seus antepassados enquanto tropeça na política da província. Eça de Queirós publicou-o em livro em 1900. Gonçalo é fraco, simpático e, no fundo, Portugal.'],
  [31347,'Contos','Eça de Queirós','Society','portuguese','Os contos de Eça de Queirós, reunidos em 1902, depois da sua morte. Entre eles, “Singularidades de uma rapariga loura”, “José Matias” e “Civilização”, onde começou A Cidade e as Serras. Uma boa porta de entrada para quem ainda não o leu.'],
  [22015,'As Minas de Salomão','Eça de Queirós','Adventure','portuguese','O romance de Rider Haggard, contado em português por Eça de Queirós em 1891: Allan Quatermain, rebatizado Allão Quartelmar, atravessa o deserto em busca das minas do rei Salomão. Eça não se limitou a traduzir e deu-lhe muito do seu estilo. O original inglês está na sala dos Reinos Perdidos.'],
  [16425,'Amor de Perdição','Camilo Castelo Branco','Romance','portuguese','Simão Botelho e Teresa de Albuquerque apaixonam-se em Viseu, filhos de duas famílias inimigas. Camilo Castelo Branco escreveu-o preso na Cadeia da Relação do Porto e publicou-o em 1862; Simão era seu tio. É o romance mais lido de Portugal durante gerações.'],
  [17927,"A Queda d'um Anjo",'Camilo Castelo Branco','Satire','portuguese','Calisto Elói, um fidalgo de província austero e antiquado, é eleito deputado e vai para Lisboa combater a corrupção dos costumes. Lisboa acaba por convertê-lo a ele. Camilo Castelo Branco publicou-o em 1866, e é das coisas mais engraçadas que escreveu.'],
  [21406,'Novelas do Minho','Camilo Castelo Branco','Society','portuguese','Novelas curtas passadas no Minho, publicadas em fascículos entre 1875 e 1877; neste volume estão “Gracejos que matam”, “O comendador” e “O cego de Landim”. Camilo conhecia de perto aquelas aldeias, aquelas famílias e aqueles ódios. Lê-se uma por noite.'],
  [16428,'Os fidalgos da Casa Mourisca','Júlio Dinis','Society','portuguese','Uma família fidalga do Minho, orgulhosa e arruinada, é salva pelo filho mais novo, Jorge, e por um lavrador enriquecido, Tomé da Póvoa. Júlio Dinis morreu em 1871, o ano em que o livro saiu. É um romance bondoso sobre um mundo que mudava.'],
  [16443,'Uma família ingleza','Júlio Dinis','Society','portuguese','Os Whitestone, uma família de comerciantes ingleses no Porto, vistos por um portuense que os conhecia bem. Júlio Dinis publicou-o em livro em 1868, com o subtítulo “Cenas da vida do Porto”. Há chá, contabilidade e um namoro que ninguém autorizou.'],
  [29120,'A Morgadinha dos Cannaviaes','Júlio Dinis','Romance','portuguese','Henrique de Souselas, um lisboeta enfastiado, vai para uma aldeia do Minho curar-se de uma doença que é sobretudo tédio, e conhece Madalena, a morgadinha. Júlio Dinis publicou-o em 1868. A aldeia, com as suas eleições e os seus mexericos, rouba-lhe a cena.'],
  [17591,'Frei Luiz de Sousa','Almeida Garrett','Drama','portuguese','Madalena de Vilhena voltou a casar, convencida de que o primeiro marido morrera em Alcácer Quibir. Um dia chega um romeiro que diz trazer notícias dele. Almeida Garrett estreou-o em 1843, e a resposta à pergunta “Romeiro, romeiro, quem és tu?” ainda gela o teatro.'],
  [24401,'Viagens na Minha Terra','Almeida Garrett','Journey','portuguese','Uma viagem de Lisboa a Santarém que se desvia a cada página para outras coisas: política, arte, livros e a história de Joaninha, a menina dos rouxinóis. Almeida Garrett publicou-o em livro em 1846. É a viagem mais curta e mais longa da literatura portuguesa.'],
  [45966,'Eurico, o presbytero','Alexandre Herculano','History','portuguese','Na Hispânia visigoda do século VIII, o presbítero Eurico, que amava Hermengarda e não pôde casar com ela, pega em armas contra a invasão árabe como um misterioso cavaleiro negro. Alexandre Herculano publicou-o em 1844. É romantismo histórico em estado puro.'],
  [9654,'Lendas e Narrativas','Alexandre Herculano','Legend','portuguese','Lendas e histórias da Idade Média portuguesa, como “O alcaide de Santarém”, “O castelo de Faria” e “A abóbada”, sobre o mestre cego que ergueu a abóbada do mosteiro da Batalha. Alexandre Herculano reuniu-as em 1851. Este é o primeiro tomo.'],
  [20142,'Os sonetos completos de Anthero de Quental','Antero de Quental','Poetry','portuguese','Os sonetos de Antero de Quental, reunidos em 1886 com um prefácio do seu amigo Oliveira Martins. São poemas de fé perdida, de angústia e de uma serenidade conquistada a custo. Antero morreu em 1891 em Ponta Delgada, a sua cidade.'],
  [8698,'O Livro de Cesario Verde','Cesário Verde','Poetry','portuguese','Os poemas de Cesário Verde, reunidos pelo amigo Silva Pinto e publicados em 1887, um ano depois da morte do poeta, com tuberculose. Lisboa ao fim da tarde, as varinas, as lojas e os candeeiros, vistos por um poeta que era também comerciante. Aqui está “O Sentimento dum Ocidental”.'],
  [17193,'Só','António Nobre','Poetry','portuguese','O único livro que António Nobre publicou em vida, em Paris, em 1892, cheio de saudades de Coimbra, do Porto e do mar. É melancólico, íntimo e cheio de nomes de terras portuguesas. O poeta morreu de tuberculose em 1900, com trinta e dois anos.'],
  [17610,'Livro de Máguas','Florbela Espanca','Poetry','portuguese','O primeiro livro de Florbela Espanca, de 1919: sonetos de amor, de solidão e de uma saudade sem objeto. Florbela escreveu como poucos sobre o que é querer demais. Morreu em 1930, no dia em que fazia trinta e seis anos.'],
  [39618,'Humus','Raul Brandão','Philosophy','portuguese','Uma vila pequena e cinzenta, velhas que esperam a morte e um diário que pergunta o que é viver. Raul Brandão publicou-o em 1917, e não se parece com nada que se escrevia então em Portugal. É um livro estranho, sombrio e inesquecível.'],
  // ---------- the Chinese Reading Room (international-wing.js): texts in Chinese, notes in traditional Chinese ----------
  [24264,'紅樓夢','曹雪芹 (Cao Xueqin)','Society','chinese','賈府的興衰，寫在一塊補天不成的石頭上，也寫在寶玉、黛玉和寶釵之間。曹雪芹生前只留下八十回的稿本，後四十回一般認為出自他人之手。讀者為它爭論了兩百多年，至今還沒有停。'],
  [23950,'三國志演義','羅貫中 (Luo Guanzhong)','Epic','chinese','從桃園結義到三分歸晉，將近一百年的戰爭、計謀和友情。羅貫中根據陳壽的《三國志》和民間說書寫成，所謂「七分事實，三分虛構」。諸葛亮的智慧、關羽的義氣和曹操的奸雄，大多是從這本書裡來的。'],
  [23962,'西遊記','吳承恩 (Wu Cheng\'en)','Legend','chinese','唐僧往西天取經，一路上有孫悟空、豬八戒和沙僧保護，九九八十一難，一難也不能少。故事本於玄奘法師真實的旅程，卻變成了一部充滿妖怪與笑話的奇書。傳統上認為作者是吳承恩，但學者至今仍有爭論。'],
  [23863,'水滸傳','施耐庵 (Shi Nai\'an)','Epic','chinese','一百零八位好漢，因為各種冤屈和意外，先後落草梁山泊。武松打虎、林沖風雪山神廟、魯智深倒拔垂楊柳，都是中國人從小聽到大的故事。書中的英雄快意恩仇，也殺人不眨眼，讀的時候不必全都佩服。'],
  [24032,'儒林外史','吳敬梓 (Wu Jingzi)','Satire','chinese','一部寫讀書人的諷刺小說：有人為了科舉考到頭髮白了，有人中了舉就喜極而瘋。吳敬梓出身望族，後來家道中落，寫的都是他熟悉的人。范進中舉那一段，至今還在課本裡。'],
  [51828,'聊齋志異','蒲松齡 (Pu Songling)','Ghosts','chinese','近五百篇短短的故事，裡面的狐仙、鬼魂和花妖，往往比人還有情有義。蒲松齡一輩子考不上舉人，在鄉下教書，據說還在路邊擺茶，請過路人講奇聞。「寫鬼寫妖高人一等，刺貪刺虐入骨三分」，是郭沫若對它的評語。'],
  [23818,'鏡花緣','李汝珍 (Li Ruzhen)','Legend','chinese','唐敖出海遊歷，經過君子國、女兒國、兩面國和大人國，每個國家都像一面哈哈鏡。後半部寫一百位才女赴考，談琴棋書畫，也談音韻和算學。李汝珍是個學問很雜的人，這本書就是他的百寶箱。'],
  [23850,'老殘遊記','劉鶚 (Liu E)','Society','chinese','一位搖著串鈴的江湖郎中，一路行醫，一路看清末山東的官場和人情。劉鶚最痛恨的不是貪官，而是自以為清廉、因此更加殘忍的「清官」。大明湖和白妞說書那幾段，寫景寫聲，都是名篇。'],
  [23910,'封神演義','陸西星 (Lu Xixing)','Legend','chinese','商紂無道，姜子牙奉命封神，神仙分成兩派，各顯法寶，打得天翻地覆。哪吒鬧海、楊戩變化和姜太公釣魚，很多人都是從這裡認識的。作者是誰，至今沒有定論，有人說是許仲琳，有人說是陸西星。'],
  [25192,'浮生六記','沈復 (Shen Fu)','Essays','chinese','一個清代的普通讀書人，寫他和妻子芸娘的日子：插花、品茶、布置小屋，也寫貧困、誤會和別離。原來有六記，流傳下來的只有四記。林語堂說芸娘是中國文學中最可愛的女人。'],
  [27166,'吶喊','魯迅 (Lu Xun)','Modern','chinese','魯迅的第一本小說集，收了〈狂人日記〉、〈孔乙己〉、〈故鄉〉和〈阿Q正傳〉。他在自序裡說起一間「鐵屋子」：裡面的人都熟睡了，要不要把他們叫醒？中國的現代小說，大致從這裡開始。'],
  [24042,'徬徨','魯迅 (Lu Xun)','Modern','chinese','魯迅的第二本小說集，比《吶喊》更安靜，也更悲涼。〈祝福〉裡的祥林嫂，一遍又一遍地講她的阿毛，沒有人再願意聽。〈傷逝〉寫一對自由戀愛的年輕人，後來怎樣走散。'],
  [25271,'朝花夕拾','魯迅 (Lu Xun)','Modern','chinese','魯迅回憶童年和青年的散文：百草園裡的蟋蟀和何首烏，三味書屋的先生，還有日本的藤野先生。他說這些是「從記憶中抄出來的」。這是他寫得最溫柔的一本書。'],
  [25242,'野草','魯迅 (Lu Xun)','Poetry','chinese','二十幾篇散文詩，寫夢、寫影子、寫求乞者和過客。〈秋夜〉開頭那句「在我的後園，可以看見牆外有兩株樹，一株是棗樹，還有一株也是棗樹」，許多人都背得出來。這是魯迅最晦澀、也最個人的作品。'],
  [25559,'中國小說史略','魯迅 (Lu Xun)','History','chinese','魯迅在北京大學講課的講義，從神話傳說一直講到清末的譴責小說。「譴責小說」這個名稱就是他取的。要知道這間閱覽室裡的小說從哪裡來，這本書是最好的導遊。'],
  [23839,'論語','孔子 (Confucius)','Philosophy','chinese','孔子和弟子的談話，由弟子和再傳弟子記錄下來，一共二十篇。「學而時習之」「三人行，必有我師焉」，兩千五百年來說了又說。讀原文會發現，孔子其實常常開玩笑，也常常嘆氣。'],
  [24178,'孟子','孟子 (Mencius)','Philosophy','chinese','孟子周遊列國，對國君講仁政，對論敵毫不客氣。「民為貴，社稷次之，君為輕」，是兩千多年前說出來的話。他相信人性本善，並用一個快要掉進井裡的孩子來說明。'],
  [7337,'道德經','老子 (Laozi)','Philosophy','chinese','五千來字，八十一章，是翻譯成外文次數最多的中文書之一。「道可道，非常道」，開頭第一句就讓讀者停下來。它講的是柔弱勝剛強，無為而無不為。'],
  [23864,'孫子兵法','孫子 (Sunzi)','Philosophy','chinese','十三篇，講的是打仗，最推崇的卻是不戰而屈人之兵。「知彼知己，百戰不殆」，今天商學院和球隊教練也愛引用。孫武是春秋時人，這本書成於何時，學者仍在討論。'],
  [23873,'詩經','佚名 (Anonymous)','Poetry','chinese','中國最早的詩歌總集，三百零五篇，大多是兩千五百多年前的民歌和宮廷樂歌。第一篇〈關雎〉寫一個睡不著覺的男子，想念河邊採荇菜的女子。孔子說讀詩可以興、觀、群、怨，也可以多認識鳥獸草木之名。'],
  [25501,'易經','佚名 (Anonymous)','Philosophy','chinese','六十四卦，每卦六爻，原是占卜的書，後來成了儒家的經典。歷代讀書人在裡面讀出了天文、政治和人生。今天打開它，仍然可以問一個問題，看看它怎麼回答。'],
  [24226,'史記','司馬遷 (Sima Qian)','History','chinese','從黃帝一直寫到漢武帝，一百三十篇，是中國第一部紀傳體通史。司馬遷為李陵辯護，受了宮刑，忍辱把書寫完。刺客、遊俠和商人都有傳，魯迅稱它為「史家之絕唱，無韻之離騷」。'],
  [24047,'世說新語','劉義慶 (Liu Yiqing)','Essays','chinese','魏晉名士的言行，一段只有幾行字：王子猷雪夜訪友，到了門口又回去，因為「乘興而行，興盡而返」。劉義慶是南朝宋的宗室，和門下的文人一起編成這本書。一千多則小故事，是中國最早的名人軼事，也是最雅的。'],
  [25288,'山海經','佚名 (Anonymous)','Legend','chinese','一本古老的地理書，記錄了山川和礦物，也記錄了九尾狐、精衛和刑天。有些怪獸一個頭三個身子，有些國家的人胸口有個洞。魯迅小時候最想要的，就是一部繪圖的《山海經》。'],
  [52323,'唐诗三百首','蘅塘退士 (Hengtangtuishi)','Poetry','chinese','清代孫洙（號蘅塘退士）從數萬首唐詩中選出三百來首，當作家塾的課本。「熟讀唐詩三百首，不會吟詩也會吟」，就是他序裡引的俗語。李白、杜甫、王維都在，一夜讀不完，一生也讀不膩。'],
  [24060,'李太白集','李白 (Li Bai)','Poetry','chinese','李白的詩集。「床前明月光」「天生我材必有用」「桃花潭水深千尺」，隨手一翻就是熟悉的句子。賀知章叫他謫仙人，他也就喝酒、求仙、漫遊，一生都不太安分。'],
  [25367,'漱玉詞','李清照 (Li Qingzhao)','Poetry','chinese','宋代最出色的女詞人。前半生和丈夫趙明誠收藏金石書畫，南渡以後國破家亡，詞也從「綠肥紅瘦」變成了「尋尋覓覓，冷冷清清」。她的作品散佚很多，這是後人輯錄的集子。'],
  [23849,'牡丹亭','湯顯祖 (Tang Xianzu)','Drama','chinese','杜麗娘在花園裡做了一個夢，夢見一個書生，醒來相思而死，三年後又為愛復生。湯顯祖在題詞裡說：「情不知所起，一往而深。」他和莎士比亞死在同一年。'],
  [23906,'西廂記','王實甫 (Wang Shifu)','Drama','chinese','書生張生在寺廟裡遇見崔鶯鶯，靠著丫鬟紅娘穿針引線，有情人終成眷屬。「紅娘」這個名字，後來就成了媒人的代稱。《紅樓夢》裡寶玉和黛玉偷偷一起讀的，就是這本。'],
  [24234,'桃花扇','孔尚任 (Kong Shangren)','Drama','chinese','明朝滅亡前後，侯方域和秦淮名妓李香君的故事，扇子上的血點被畫成了桃花。孔尚任是孔子的後代，他說這齣戲是「借離合之情，寫興亡之感」。結尾兩人重逢，卻雙雙入道，沒有團圓。'],
  [52270,'長生殿','洪昇 (Hong Sheng)','Drama','chinese','唐明皇和楊貴妃的故事，從七夕的誓言，到馬嵬坡的死別，再到月宮的重逢。洪昇寫了十多年，三易其稿。據說因為在國喪期間演出，他和許多看戲的人都受了處分。'],
  [24004,'竇娥寃','關漢卿 (Guan Hanqing)','Drama','chinese','一個年輕的寡婦被冤枉殺人，臨刑前發下三樁誓願：血飛白練、六月飛雪、大旱三年，後來一一應驗。「六月飛雪」從此成了冤屈的代名詞。關漢卿是元代最重要的劇作家，一生寫了六十多種雜劇。'],
  [25245,'子不語','袁枚 (Yuan Mei)','Ghosts','chinese','書名來自《論語》的「子不語怪力亂神」：孔子不談的，袁枚偏偏要談。幾百則鬼怪故事，常常帶著幾分調侃。袁枚是乾隆年間的名士，四十歲不到就辭官，住進了南京的隨園。'],
  [23817,'閱微草堂筆記','紀昀 (Ji Yun)','Ghosts','chinese','紀曉嵐晚年寫下的狐鬼故事和見聞，一千多則，文字簡淡。他是《四庫全書》的總纂官，讀過的書比誰都多。和《聊齋》不同，他的鬼常常在講道理。'],
  [25401,'陶庵夢憶','張岱 (Zhang Dai)','Essays','chinese','明朝亡了以後，張岱躲進山裡，回憶從前的繁華：看燈、看戲、品茶，雪夜去湖心亭看雪。他自己說，這些往事像一場夢。〈湖心亭看雪〉一篇不到二百字，是中國散文裡最安靜的一場雪。'],
  [7420,'幽夢影','張潮 (Zhang Chao)','Philosophy','chinese','一本清言小品，每則只有一兩句，像「花不可以無蝶，山不可以無泉」。張潮的朋友們在旁邊寫下評語，有時比原文還好玩。適合睡前隨手翻幾頁。'],
  [24050,'菜根譚','洪自誠 (Hong Zicheng)','Philosophy','chinese','明代洪應明（字自誠）寫的處世格言，書名一般認為取自「咬得菜根，則百事可做」。儒家、道家、佛家的道理，都化成了對仗工整的短句。它在日本也很流行，不少人放在案頭。'],
  [7406,'茶經','陸羽 (Lu Yu)','Essays','chinese','世界上第一部關於茶的專著，從茶樹、茶具一直寫到煮茶和飲茶。陸羽是個棄嬰，在寺院裡長大，後來被尊為「茶聖」。讀這本書最好的時候，當然是泡一壺茶的時候。'],
  [24141,'警世通言','馮夢龍 (Feng Menglong)','Society','chinese','「三言」之一，四十篇白話短篇小說。白娘子永鎮雷峰塔、杜十娘怒沉百寶箱，都在這裡。馮夢龍把宋元明以來的說書故事收集、改寫，編成了這部書。'],
  [25349,'東周列國志','馮夢龍 (Feng Menglong)','History','chinese','從周宣王一直寫到秦始皇統一天下，五百多年的春秋戰國，一百零八回。臥薪嘗膽、完璧歸趙、荊軻刺秦，一個接一個。原是馮夢龍的《新列國志》，清代蔡元放修訂後，改成了現在的名字。'],
  [26872,'海上花列傳','韓邦慶 (Han Bangqing)','Society','chinese','清末上海租界裡的長三書寓，一群妓女和她們的客人，吃酒、打牌、爭風吃醋。對白用的是吳語，張愛玲後來把它譯成了國語和英文。胡適說它是「吳語文學的第一部傑作」。'],
  [24138,'官場現形記','李伯元 (Li Boyuan)','Satire','chinese','清末官場的眾生相：買官、賣官、送禮、鑽營，一個故事接著一個，像一串連起來的笑話。李伯元在上海辦報，這部小說就在他自己的報紙上連載。魯迅把它和另外三部歸為「譴責小說」。'],
  [24099,'二十年目睹之怪現狀','吳趼人 (Wu Jianren)','Satire','chinese','一個自號「九死一生」的年輕人，二十年間在各地遇見的，只有蛇蟲鼠蟻、豺狼虎豹和魑魅魍魎。吳趼人用第一人稱來寫，這在當時的長篇小說裡還很少見。書裡的騙子和怪事，今天讀來仍然眼熟。'],
  [25376,'三俠五義','石玉崑 (Shi Yukun)','Adventure','chinese','包公斷案，展昭、白玉堂等俠客相助，是中國武俠小說的老祖宗之一。石玉崑是道光年間的說書藝人，書是從他的說唱整理出來的。錦毛鼠白玉堂闖沖霄樓那一段，不知讓多少讀者掉過眼淚。'],
  [24112,'人間詞話','王國維 (Wang Guowei)','Poetry','chinese','王國維論詞的札記，提出了「境界」之說。最有名的是古今成大事業、大學問者必經的三種境界，借三首宋詞的句子說出來。篇幅不長，卻影響了一百年的文學批評。'],
  [25273,'天工開物','宋應星 (Song Yingxing)','Essays','chinese','明代的一部技術百科，寫種稻、織布、燒瓷、鑄錢、造船，一直寫到採珠和製墨。宋應星六次赴京會試都沒考上，他在序裡說，這本書「於功名進取，毫不相關也」。書在中國一度失傳，後來從日本找了回來。'],
  [12479,'三字經','佚名 (Anonymous)','Wonder','chinese','「人之初，性本善」，三個字一句，好念好背，幾百年來是中國孩子的第一本書。裡面講歷史、講經書，也講勤學的故事。作者一般認為是宋代的王應麟，但沒有定論。'],
  [23912,'千字文','周興嗣 (Zhou Xingsi)','Wonder','chinese','一千個字，幾乎沒有一個重複，從「天地玄黃，宇宙洪荒」開始。據說梁武帝從王羲之的字帖中拓出一千個不同的字，讓周興嗣編成韻文，他一夜編成，頭髮都白了。一千多年來，它是孩子們認字、書法家練字的範本。']
];
